{
  "email": "mei.chen@brightline.example",
  "reason": "Ticket sender reports Excel shows Unlicensed Product and editing is restricted. Checking account status and devices per KB-026."
}

{
  "outcome": "needs_user_info",
  "reply_to_user": "Hi Mei, thanks for reporting this. Your account is active and not locked, so this is most likely an Office sign-in issue rather than a password problem. Please try these steps: in Excel, go to File, then Account, then Sign out. Close all Office apps, reopen Excel, and sign in with your company email (mei.chen@brightline.example), not a personal Microsoft account. Then check whether the Unlicensed Product banner is gone. If it still shows after you have signed in with your company email, reply here with a screenshot of the banner and the exact account shown under File, then Account, and we will escalate it so the license can be checked.",
  "internal_note": "Checked lookup_user for user_id 9 (Mei Chen, Operations, Logistics Analyst). Account active, not blocked, failed_login_count 0, password set 2026-09-11, no must-change flag. Device on file: BL-LT-0109. Followed KB-026 quick checks: account is not locked and no recent reset is evident. Have not confirmed which Office account she is signed into. Asked user to run the KB-026 sign-out and sign-in fix and to report back. If the banner persists after a correct company sign-in, escalate to Tier 2 to verify the Microsoft 365 license assignment (KB-026 escalate condition). Side note: mailbox is 99.4% full (50.9 GB of 51.2 GB). This is not the cause of the license banner but may cause send/receive issues, so consider a mailbox cleanup or quota review.",
  "kb_articles": [
    "KB-026"
  ],
  "confidence": "medium"
}
